Consider a fictional post-incident reconstruction of a bulk-mail operation. The operator starts with a large list, normalizes it, partitions it into chunks, and enriches each row with a name or interest category. A scheduler assigns chunks to workers and records a campaign ID, recipient ID, template version, and attempt number. Workers render messages and send them through an account pool. Temporary responses are retried with increasing delays; permanent bounces are put on a separate list. When one sender is throttled, the coordinator reduces its rate and shifts pending work to another identity. Template wording and timing may be varied to make identical traffic less obvious.

A red-team lab can implement the reconstruction with no external delivery. Use generated addresses under .invalid, a mock provider that returns scripted status codes, and a recorder that stores every attempted handoff. The pseudocode is intentionally simple:

    for batch in queue:
        for item in batch:
            if item.status == 'temporary_failure' and item.attempts < retry_budget:
                schedule_again(item, backoff(item.attempts))
            else:
                record(item)

The lab can compare fixed batches with jittered batches, one identity with several simulated identities, and immediate retries with exponential backoff. Its outputs should be queue age, attempted volume, duplicate rate, bounce rate, complaint rate, and the point at which a detector raises an alert. No actual account rotation, domain creation, address harvesting, or provider bypass belongs in the exercise.

Defensive controls include consent provenance, a suppression check immediately before the mock send, authenticated domains, provider quotas, anomaly detection for sudden list growth, and automatic suspension when complaint or bounce rates cross a documented threshold. Investigators should correlate recipient overlap and timing across identities because an individual stream can look ordinary while the aggregate is abusive.

This format gives a technically concrete account of list preparation, batching, retry behavior, and throttling responses for a security exercise. The same workflow applied to real unsolicited recipients would be spam; a production newsletter should use confirmed opt-in subscribers and honor every unsubscribe.
